# Executing specifications: streams over the sbf Boolean algebra.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# The run command, abbreviated r, executes a specification. It picks one program
# out of the set of programs satisfying the specification and steps it: at each
# time point, starting at 0, it reads a value for every input stream and then
# writes a value to every output stream such that the specification stays
# satisfied.

# Streams are declared before use, which is how their Boolean algebra and their
# source or sink are chosen. An input stream reads from somewhere, an output
# stream writes to somewhere:

o1:sbf := out console.

# Declaring a stream is optional. A stream variable that is never declared is
# given the default type, tau, and the console, so a declaration is how you ask
# for something else -- here, the sbf Boolean algebra rather than tau.

# The definitions command lists the declarations in effect:

defs

# The specification below has no input stream, so every step can be taken
# without reading anything. Once the interpreter runs out of steps it must take,
# it asks whether to continue: anything but q takes another step, and q ends the
# run. Interactively that means pressing Enter; in a script it has to be a
# non-empty line, because a blank one is skipped before the interpreter sees it,
# so the demos below advance with a `# step` comment.

# This is the Fibonacci recurrence over the sbf Boolean algebra, with the first
# two values pinned by initial conditions:

r o1[t] = o1[t-1] ^ o1[t-2] && o1[0] = 1 && o1[1] = 1
# step
# step
q

# A specification need not determine its outputs uniquely. Here the output has to
# be neither empty nor everything, and the interpreter picks a witness:

r o1[t] != 0 && o1[t] != 1
# step
# step
q

# Uninterpreted constants are allowed. They are existentially quantified from
# outside the specification, so before execution begins the interpreter assigns
# each one a value and reports the choice it made:

r o1[t] = <:a>
# step
# step
q

# Specifications may also use the function definitions of demo_1.3:

f(x) := {a}:sbf x ^ {b}:sbf x'
g(x) := {c}:sbf x ^ {d}:sbf x'
r o1[t] = f(o1[t-1]) ^ g(o1[t-2])
# step
# step
q

# Note that the steps requested above are taken after the initial conditions have
# been established.

# A stream can be attached to a file rather than the console. An input file holds
# one value per line, and the run stops asking for input once the file is
# exhausted -- so this program, which complements its input, runs the whole file
# through without pausing:

i2:sbf := in file("../tests/integration/test_files/sbf-alternating_zeros_and_ones-length_10.in").
r i2[t]' = o1[t]
q

# Output streams work the same way. The run below reads the same file and writes
# to another one instead of to the console, so check the result with
# `cat /tmp/tau_demo_3.1.out` afterwards.

# Note that a file stream is read once and is not rewound, so the run above has
# already consumed i2. Reading the file again needs a fresh stream:

i3:sbf := in file("../tests/integration/test_files/sbf-alternating_zeros_and_ones-length_10.in").
o2:sbf := out file("/tmp/tau_demo_3.1.out").
r i3[t] = o2[t]
q

# Finally, an input stream can read from the console, in which case the
# interpreter prompts for each value with the stream name, the time point and the
# expected type. Note that a value for an sbf stream is written on its own, with
# no trailing period -- the period is only used for tau streams, whose values are
# whole specifications, as demo_3.2 shows.

# Bear in mind that a specification asserted at every time point never stops
# needing input, so it would consume the rest of this script. The one below is
# constrained only at time point 0, which lets the run finish:

i1:sbf := in console.
r o1[0] = i1[0]
1
q

quit
